## Complete Basic xCAT Setup for *Head Node*

With xCAT installed, register the internal provisioning interface with xCAT for
DHCP service. The head node serves DNS to the cluster (`makedns`, below), so it
is the compute nodes' name server and forwards other lookups to
`${dns_servers}`. Then set the cluster domain name used for system-wide name
resolution; `makedns` requires the head node to resolve by its name in that
domain, so add it to `/etc/hosts`.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Complete basic xCAT setup for head node -->
```bash
# Verify required variables are set
: "${sms_eth_internal:?ERROR: sms_eth_internal not set}"
: "${sms_ip:?ERROR: sms_ip not set}"

# Register internal provisioning interface with xCAT for DHCP
chdef -t site dhcpinterfaces="xcatmn|${sms_eth_internal}"

# Point provisioning services at the internal interface address. Without
# this, a multi-homed head node may auto-select its external address.
# The head node is the cluster's name server; it forwards other names upstream.
chdef -t site master=${sms_ip} nameservers=${sms_ip} forwarders=${dns_servers}

# Define local domain name; makedns needs the head node's name in this domain
chdef -t site domain=${dns_domain:-local}
echo "${sms_ip} ${sms_name}.${dns_domain:-local}" >> /etc/hosts
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->
